window.docContents[142].resolve({"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-digital-transmission-built-by-the-kind-algebra/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-digital-transmission-built-by-the-kind-algebra":{"contents":"Several Part-13 definitions state a quantity's defining relation algebraically. The signal\nenergy per binary digit is the product of carrier power and the bit period (13-19,\nE_bit = P_c·T_bit); the periods are reciprocals of the rates (13-12, 13-14). Each is\nspecified as an R12 kind-law.\n\n\n\nSignal energy per bit is carrier power × bit period — a verified construction (item\n13-19). A signal energy built as the product of a carrier power and a bit period carries its\nproduct certificate by construction, over the ℝ carrier. The companion\nsignalEnergy_dim_from_power_period shows it is M·L²·T⁻² because it is a power times a\ntime. Uses the quotient kind-law.\n\nsignalEnergyOf p t := Quantity.mul signalEnergy_prod_power_period p t, and\nsignalEnergyOf_isProduct is rfl. The reciprocal kind-laws\nperiodOfDataElements_recip_transferRate and periodOfBinaryDigits_recip_binaryDigitRate\ngive the periods, with periodOfDataElements_dim_from_transferRate the dimension. Axiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","header":"31.4. The algebraic Remarks as kind-laws: digital transmission built by the kind algebra","id":"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-digital-transmission-built-by-the-kind-algebra/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--The-algebraic-Remarks-as-kind-laws___-digital-transmission-built-by-the-kind-algebra"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--AI-assistance-contract":{"contents":"* Use the HCP-owned Body, Spring, PartOf, and CoupledPair types; do not import or reuse the Tutorial model.* Keep body mass, spring stiffness, and pair normal-mode frequency on their respective bearers.* Give each spring a signed displacement-kind extension (negative when compressed, positive when stretched), and compare configurations under the same PairId.* Treat the pair as a whole distinct from every body and spring part.* State that the L1 no-interaction assumption falls here and that L3 revisits the on-axis, rigid-body assumption.* Use sort-specific Assembles licenses: mass for the pair and stiffness for parallel springs, but neither angular frequency for the pair nor stiffness for springs in series.* Treat guarded elaboration errors as evidence only for the refusals they show.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.8. AI assistance contract","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--AI-assistance-contract"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--AI-assistance-contract":{"contents":"* Keep L6 independent, with local nominal PairId | P | Q, Body | A | B, and the\n  coupling spring.* Preserve nominal identity: one pair name is one system; same-name records are comparable\n  states or configurations; P and Q are distinct.* Keep pair mass on the pair whole and body masses on their body-in-pair bearers.* Use the pairS sort scope for R1 and the singleton object scope containing P for empirical R3.* Mark the rated limit as an external referent; make the gate mention it and evaluate it on\n  two states of P.* Pin both invalid scopes and run the requirement survey, coverage census, and clean gate.* Do not import the Tutorial worked example. The full worked example is\n  examples/Tutorial/HarmonicOscillatorRequirements.lean.* Leave optional .decided scope out of this lesson. If used, provide a deciderProof\n  theorem proving that its decider returns true for every result of the governed member;\n  without that proof the census reports the port unchecked.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.8. AI assistance contract","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--AI-assistance-contract"}});